{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"colourable-hillocky-blackmamba","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# S7b — Run the LOCAL developer gates on Bedrock (dogfood before the production cutover)\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nS8 (eb6e) cuts the PRODUCTION review bot over to Bedrock with NO fallback — it is the gate that votes\non every landing on `main`. Today the first time rebar exercises Bedrock through a real gate will be\nthat cutover, on the shared bot, where a failure stops everyone's landings until a human runs the kill\nswitch.\n\nLocal developer gates (`rebar review-plan`, `rebar review-code`, `rebar verify-completion`) run the\nSAME LLM stack against the SAME provider seam, on a machine where a failure costs one person one\ncommand. Pointing them at Bedrock first converts the production cutover from a first contact into a\nconfirmation.\n\n## What is already established — do NOT re-measure\n\n* BEDROCK IS INVOKABLE from this host: account 896586841071, us-east-1, caller\n  `arn:aws:iam::896586841071:user/joe_frontier`. AWS CLI credentials are present and working.\n* ONLY INFERENCE PROFILES WORK. Plain on-demand ids (`anthropic.claude-*`) are NOT invokable in this\n  account at all — they return a ValidationException directing you to use an inference profile\n  [rebar:2932-8a98-6d70-4550].\n* PROMPT CACHING WORKS on a regional inference profile: `us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6` measured\n  cache_write=4017 then cached on the next attempt; a longer end-to-end run recorded\n  cache_read=542,723 / cache_write=6,239 over 19 calls. Caching and inference profiles are NOT\n  mutually exclusive, and `us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6` is the recorded verified caching default.\n* `temperature=0` is REFUSED on opus (`ValidationException: temperature is deprecated for this\n  model`) and is already handled by the `supports_temperature: False` capability override — the\n  withdrawal is MODEL-driven, not provider-driven, and happens identically on both providers.\n* PAYLOAD AND TOOL PARITY ARE PROVEN [rebar:0d76-ebbf-ee3a-48ac]: for the same logical call the system\n  prompt, messages and tool definitions are byte-identical across providers; the model id is the only\n  differing field. Eight envelope differences are enumerated there, seven of them spelling.\n* ONE ENVELOPE DIFFERENCE IS SUBSTANTIVE and is the thing most likely to bite here: Bedrock's\n  `cachePoint` carries NO TTL where Anthropic's `cache_control` carries `ttl:\"5m\"`. Latent today\n  because rebar sets no non-default TTL — but this story is the cheapest place to find out if that\n  assumption is wrong.\n\n## Approach\n\nConfigure the LOCAL rebar install to resolve its model classes to Bedrock inference profiles, using the\nAWS CLI credentials already on the host, and then RUN THE REAL GATES and compare against the Anthropic\nbaseline. Prefer the layered config file (`REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` / the `model_classes` table) over the\ndeprecated bare `REBAR_LLM_MODEL`, so the change is per-class and reversible rather than global.\n\nThe deliverable is not the config line — it is the EVIDENCE that a developer's day-to-day gates behave\non Bedrock: verdicts still parse, structured output still validates, caching still engages, and the\nobservable cost does not regress.\n\n**Explicitly out of scope:** the production bot, its IAM, its alarms, and its kill switch. Those are\nS8's. This story must not touch `infra/`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- local LLM configuration only (the config file and/or documented env for a developer machine)\n- `docs/local-dev-env.md` — how a developer opts in, and how to revert\n- no `src/` behaviour change is expected; if one turns out to be required, that is a finding worth a\n  separate ticket rather than silent scope growth\n\n## Testing\n\nLive, because a configuration that only works in a unit test proves nothing here. The oracle is a real\nlocal gate run against a real ticket.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] [operator-attested] `rebar review-plan` runs end-to-end against Bedrock on a real ticket and\n      returns a parseable verdict, recorded with the ticket id, the resolved model ids per pass, and\n      the run timestamp.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] the run's `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` per-call tally shows EVERY call resolved to a\n      Bedrock inference profile and ZERO calls to direct Anthropic — the same provider-clean check S6\n      applied to its measured arms.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] prompt caching is observed ENGAGING on the Bedrock path: a run records\n      nonzero `cache_read_tokens`, cited from the usage log. A write alone is insufficient — write-only\n      is the failure mode bug f81d exists for.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] `rebar review-code` and `rebar verify-completion` are each exercised once on\n      Bedrock and their outcomes recorded. These are the other two gates a developer hits daily; a\n      plan-review-only proof would leave them untested.\n- [ ] Structured output validates on every gate run — no verdict falls back to an unparsed or\n      INDETERMINATE shape for a provider-shaped reason. Any that does is recorded with its raw payload.\n- [ ] Whether the missing Bedrock `cachePoint` TTL causes any observable difference is STATED, with\n      evidence either way. \"Not observed\" is an acceptable answer; silence is not.\n- [ ] The opt-in and the REVERT are both documented in `docs/local-dev-env.md`, and the revert is\n      executed once to prove a developer can get back to Anthropic in one step.\n- [ ] Nothing under `infra/` is modified — verified by the diff.\n- [ ] make lint and make typecheck pass by exit code; the default selection is green.","id":"1aa2-37cf-5cdf-48b7","parent_id":"061c-ecd1-8967-4a76","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"S7b — Run the LOCAL developer gates on Bedrock (dogfood before the production cutover)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785728959906700001,"uuid":"59d9a3c2-d04d-4892-a37f-845674b3f8d7"}